# Deploying vid2sql on AWS (owner steps)

Nothing in this folder has been deployed. The template, the two Lambda entry
points and the image recipe are prepared and the entry points are covered by
offline tests (`tests/test_lambda_handlers.py`, an in-memory S3 stand-in).
The CloudFormation template itself has not been validated by AWS yet; step 2
does that.

## What gets created

| Resource | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `UploadBucket` (S3, private, encrypted) | videos go under `uploads/`; objects expire after 14 days |
| `PipelineFunction` (Lambda, container image, arm64) | on each upload, runs the OpenCV 5 pipeline and merges rows into `db/v2s.db` |
| `ResultsBucket` (S3, private, versioned) | holds the SQLite database |
| `QueryFunction` + `QueryApi` (Lambda + HTTP API) | the web endpoint: SQL box, plain questions, timestamp links |

The function image is built from `deploy/Dockerfile` with the dependencies
pinned in `deploy/requirements-lambda.txt`. No secrets or API keys are used.

## Steps

1. Install the AWS SAM CLI and Docker on a machine signed in to the target
   AWS account (region of your choice).
2. From the repository root, validate: `sam validate --template deploy/template.yaml --lint`
3. Build the images: `sam build --template deploy/template.yaml`
4. Deploy: `sam deploy --guided --stack-name vid2sql` (accept the defaults;
   allow SAM to create the image repository when asked).
5. Copy the `WebEndpoint`, `UploadBucketName` and `ResultsBucketName` values
   from the stack outputs.
6. Upload a clip (set its licence in the object metadata so it is recorded):
   `aws s3 cp data/clips/clip3_cobbled.webm s3://<UploadBucketName>/uploads/ --metadata license="CC BY 3.0",source-url="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Traffic_on_a_cobbled_street_-_soundscape.webm"`
7. Wait about two minutes, then open `WebEndpoint` and ask "How many moving
   objects are in each video?". Until the first upload finishes the endpoint
   answers 503 "no database yet".
8. For the submission, record a screen capture of steps 6-7 as proof the AWS
   component works, and put the `WebEndpoint` URL in the submission form.

## Known risks to check on first deploy

- Image size and cold start: the OpenCV image may need more than the default
  build time; the pipeline function allows 15 minutes and 4 GB of scratch
  disk, enough for a one-minute 1080p clip (about 1 minute of processing in
  local runs on a shared server; Lambda speed is untested).
- The first `sam deploy` needs permission to create IAM roles, S3 buckets,
  an ECR repository, Lambda functions and an HTTP API.
- The HTTP API is public and throttled (10 requests/second). Queries are
  read-only, but add an authorizer before leaving it up long-term.
- To avoid cost, delete the stack when judging is over: `sam delete`.
